<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hashimoto Sanai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1859">1859</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hashimoto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanai">Sanai</a> was a Japanese physician and reformist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fukui">Fukui</a> domain. Trained in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> medicine and Western learning, he became an adviser to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsudaira-yoshinaga">Matsudaira Yoshinaga</a> and argued for opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, strengthening its defences and choosing a capable shogunal heir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a>. He was executed in 1859 in the Ansei Purge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>'s chief minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ii-naosuke">Ii Naosuke</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hashimoto-sanai.jpg" alt="Hashimoto Sanai" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bokusen Shimada · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hashimoto_sanai_bokusen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
